<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Library of Alexandria</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> was the great library founded under the Ptolemaic kings in Alexandria in the third century BCE, part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mouseion">Mouseion</a> research institution. It aimed to collect all known Greek writing and became the leading centre of scholarship in the Hellenistic world. It is one of the most celebrated legacies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/library-of-alexandria.jpg" alt="Library of Alexandria" width="320" height="325" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: O. Von Corven · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancientlibraryalex.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The library was part of a larger research institution, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mouseion">Mouseion</a>, dedicated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muses">Muses</a>. Libraries were not new: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> kept an archive of written materials around 3400 BCE, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/library-of-nineveh">Library of Ashurbanipal</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a> was founded in the seventh century BCE. The idea for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> may have been proposed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I Soter</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-of-phalerum">Demetrius of Phalerum</a>, an exiled Athenian statesman, but sources disagree about the founding, and the library itself was probably not built until the reign of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-ii-philadelphus">Ptolemy II Philadelphus</a>.</p><p>The Ptolemaic kings meant it to hold all knowledge. They sent royal agents with large sums to buy as many texts as possible, favouring older copies. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galen">Galen</a>, books found on ships entering the port were copied by official scribes and the copies returned to their owners. How many scrolls it held at any time is unknown, with estimates from 40,000 to 700,000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Scholars at the library belonged to no philosophical school, but they answered to the king. Zenodotus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> worked on the text of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callimachus">Callimachus</a> wrote the Pinakes, sometimes called the first library catalogue, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eratosthenes">Eratosthenes of Cyrene</a>, the third head librarian, calculated the circumference of the Earth. A daughter library was set up in the Serapeum under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iii-euergetes">Ptolemy III Euergetes</a>.</p><p>The decline came by stages. In 145 BCE Ptolemy VIII Physcon expelled the scholars, and the head librarian Aristarchus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samothrace">Samothrace</a> went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. In 48 BCE a fire started during Caesar's siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> spread near the docks. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a>, quoting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>, says it destroyed 40,000 scrolls, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Cassius Dio</a> mentions only storehouses of books and Florus and Lucan only the fleet and some houses, so it is unclear how much was lost. Under Roman rule membership of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mouseion">Mouseion</a> came to be granted for distinction in government, the army or athletics, and it seems to have ceased by the 260s CE. The Serapeum was demolished in 391 CE under a decree of Bishop Theophilus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>By the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> almost every city in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> had a public library. Much of the library's material may have survived through the Imperial Library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, the Academy of Gondishapur and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom">House of Wisdom</a>, and was transmitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> from the 12th century. The modern Bibliotheca Alexandrina, completed in 2002, commemorates it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Library of Alexandria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The city produced notable scholars and engineers: Aristophanes of Byzantium, head of the <b>Library of Alexandria</b> around 200 BCE, edited Homer and other poets and is credited with developing the system of Greek accents, while Philo of Byzantium wrote on mechanics and siegecraft.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It was home to the <b>Library of Alexandria</b> and the Mouseion, which attracted scholars such as Euclid, Eratosthenes and Herophilus, and to the Pharos lighthouse, one of the Seven Wonders of the ancient world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ptolemaic-empire">Ptolemaic Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…Hellenistic and Roman periods, Greek was the language of science and scholarship: Euclid’s Elements of geometry, the works of Archimedes, the medical writings of Hippocrates’ school and of Galen, the astronomy of Ptolemy, and the critical scholarship of the <b>Library of Alexandria</b>, which edited and preserved the classics.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-greek">Ancient Greek · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>In Alexandria the Ptolemies founded the Museum and its great <b>Library</b>, gathering texts from across the Mediterranean world.</blockquote><a href="/essays/cross-cultural-exchange">Cross-Cultural Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>The court also kept scholars and poets: Ptolemy II paid stipends to Callimachus and Theocritus, the mathematician Euclid worked under his patronage, and the chief librarian of the <b>Library of Alexandria</b> was also the crown prince’s tutor.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ptolemaic-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ptolemaic Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
